(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-cam-nutrition-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 1 — Master Exam (Form D)","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture, when is fluoride supplementation NOT recommended for infants?","choices":["Before 6 months of age","After 1 year of age","Only during breastfeeding","Only in hot climates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: fluoride supplementation is not recommended before 6 months, and fluoride-free water should be used in formula before then. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"Per the lecture's list of food-choice influences, which is cited as the single biggest driver of food choice (\"#1\")?","choices":["Flavor, texture, and appearance","Marketing and advertising","Overall cost and economics","General nutrition content"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lecture explicitly ranks flavor, texture, and appearance as the #1 influence on food choice, ahead of early influences, habits/availability/convenience, marketing, restaurant dining, time, cost, sustainability, and nutrition. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"What daily fiber intake does the lecture recommend for adults?","choices":["25 to 35 grams per day","5 to 10 grams per day","50 to 60 grams per day","100 grams per day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the recommended fiber intake is 25 to 35 grams per day. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"According to the Food and Nutrition Board, what percent of total daily calories should come from protein?","choices":["20% to 30%","45% to 65%","20% to 35%","10% to 15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Food and Nutrition Board sets protein at about 10% to 15% of total calories, with higher-need exceptions such as endurance athletes (up to 1.7 g/kg body weight) and the elderly (1 to 1.2 g/kg). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 86 (RDA for Protein); contrast slide 35 (carbohydrate) and slides 46/66 (lipid)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what are hemorrhoids?","choices":["Diverticula that protrude through the intestinal wall","Pouches in the large intestine caused by low fiber intake","An erosion of the stomach lining","Swollen veins of the rectum and anus, also called piles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines hemorrhoids as 'swollen veins of rectum and anus; also called piles.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of enteral feeding intolerance related to delayed gastric emptying?","choices":["Physical therapy and daily ambulation sessions","Routine annual influenza vaccination","Sedation and narcotic pain medications","Daily oral multivitamin supplementation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sedation and narcotic pain medications are listed causes of delayed gastric emptying and GI dysmotility contributing to enteral feeding intolerance (along with hypothermia, sepsis, gastroparesis, ileus, recent surgery, and trauma). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does the lecture recommend treating diabetic hypoglycemia?","choices":["Restrict all carbohydrate until symptoms pass","Consume about 15 grams of simple carbohydrate, such as a glucose tablet, or give glucagon","Give a high-fat, high-protein meal and wait several hours","Administer insulin immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: diabetic hypoglycemia is treated with about 15 grams of simple carbohydrate (or glucagon). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 42"},{"q":"The 2006 Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act mandated that manufacturers clearly identify which allergens on food labels, per the lecture?","choices":["Milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, peanuts, tree nuts, wheat, and soy","Only peanuts, tree nuts, and shellfish specifically","Gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley","Sulfites, MSG, and artificial food coloring agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists these eight allergens as the major food allergens the 2006 Act required manufacturers to identify on labels. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Per the lecture, breastfeeding is associated with which long-term benefit for the infant?","choices":["Reduced risk of obesity later in life and modest improvements in cognitive performance","Accelerated uterine involution occurring after delivery of the placenta","Protection against maternal breast and ovarian cancer","Delayed return of the mother's normal ovulatory menstrual cycles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists reduced risk of obesity later in life and modest cognitive performance improvements among the infant advantages of breastfeeding. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug pair from the lecture's 'Medicine Cabinet' slide are both classified as H2 blockers rather than proton pump inhibitors?","choices":["Omeprazole and Lansoprazole","Rabeprazole and Esomeprazole","Nizatidine and Famotidine","Cimetidine and Omeprazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists Nizatidine (Axid) and Famotidine (Pepcid) as H2 blocker examples, along with Cimetidine and Ranitidine. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to limit children's screen time to about how much?","choices":["About 6 hours per day","There is no recommended limit","Less than 2 hours per day","Less than 8 hours per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites limiting screen time to less than 2 hours per day, about 14 hours per week. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 448"},{"q":"What is the RDA for iron during pregnancy, and why is it necessary according to the lecture?","choices":["27 mg/day, necessary for fetal and placental development and maternal red blood cell expansion.","11 mg/day, needed specifically to help prevent fetal growth restriction.","220 mcg/day, necessary specifically to help prevent fetal goiter formation.","600 IU/day, necessary specifically to support proper calcium absorption."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives 27 mg/day as the iron RDA in pregnancy, necessary for fetal/placental development and maternal RBC expansion. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the three key principles of the weight-loss triad, per lecture?","choices":["Restricting calories, adding physical activity, and adding an appropriate behavioral component","Intermittent fasting, whole-body detoxing, and multi-day juice cleanses","Bariatric surgery, prescription medication, and dietary supplements","Genetic testing, prepackaged meal kits, and one-on-one personal training"],"answer":0,"exp":"The weight-loss triad consists of restricting calories, adding physical activity, and adding an appropriate behavioral component, ideally with guidance from a registered dietitian nutritionist. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"During the second half of pregnancy, what happens to plasma lipids according to the lecture?","choices":["Plasma cholesterol and triglycerides both decrease noticeably","Plasma cholesterol increases about 50% and triglycerides may triple","Only plasma triglycerides rise; cholesterol stays stable","Plasma lipids remain unchanged until after delivery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states plasma cholesterol increases roughly 50% and plasma triglycerides may triple during the second half of pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"The lecture notes that introducing foods other than human milk or formula before what age is associated with a higher risk of allergic disease?","choices":["Before 6 weeks of age","Before 4 months of age","Before 1 year of age","Before 2 years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: introducing other foods before 4 months of age is associated with higher risk of allergic diseases. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 675"},{"q":"A neural tube defect in which major parts of the brain and skull fail to develop is known as:","choices":["Spina bifida","Encephalocele","Meningocele","Anencephaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines anencephaly as the condition in which major parts of the brain and skull do not develop. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"According to the lecture, until approximately what age does the body typically operate at peak performance before age-related decline begins?","choices":["About age 65 years","About age 18 years","About age 30 years","About age 50 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the body operates at peak performance until about age 30 years. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Consuming more than 60 grams of fiber per day can lead to which problem, according to the lecture?","choices":["Constipation from inadequate fluid intake","Decreased availability of some minerals","Increased risk of colon cancer","Increased cholesterol absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Too much fiber (>60 g/day) requires extra fluid, may decrease the availability of some minerals, and can create unmet energy needs in children. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 16 (Too Much Fiber); contrast slide 15 (Too Little Fiber) and slide 13 (Benefits of Fiber)"},{"q":"The lecture notes older adults often consume more fat than recommended and frequently the \"wrong\" fats. Which fats does it identify as the wrong ones to emphasize?","choices":["Saturated fats and trans fats","Unsaturated oils such as olive oil","Essential fatty acids from fish","Medium-chain plant fats"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture identifies saturated and trans fats as the wrong fats older adults often over-consume. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How does the lecture contrast animal fats with plant oils?","choices":["Both animal fats and plant oils provide 4 kilocalories per gram","Plant oils raise blood cholesterol more than animal fats","Animal fats are solid at room temperature; plant oils are liquid and contain essential fatty acids","Animal fats are liquid and plant oils are solid at room temperature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: animal fats are solid and should be limited, while plant oils are liquid and supply essential fatty acids. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 15"},{"q":"According to the lecture, why does the fiber in whole grains help with glucose control in older adults?","choices":["It converts complex carbohydrates into simple sugars","It eliminates the need for insulin entirely","It raises the glycemic index of a meal","It helps stabilize blood glucose, unlike simple carbohydrates that raise it quickly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: whole-grain fiber stabilizes glucose, while simple carbohydrates cause hyperglycemia and eventual glycosylation. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which trace mineral is a component of the thyroid hormones?","choices":["Calcium","Fluoride","Sodium","Iodine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: iodine is a component of the thyroid hormones. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 111"},{"q":"What results from positive energy balance?","choices":["Weight loss","Dehydration","No change in weight","Weight gain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Positive energy balance — intake greater than output — results in weight gain. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which best matches the lecture's definition of malnutrition?","choices":["A permanent and completely irreversible loss of lean body mass that occurs regardless of nutrition support, medical treatment, or underlying disease process.","A single micronutrient or vitamin deficiency identified incidentally on routine bloodwork, unrelated to body composition or function.","Any diagnosis of obesity in a hospitalized patient, regardless of their muscle mass, intake adequacy, or inflammatory status.","An acute, subacute, or chronic nutrition state where over- or under-nutrition, with or without inflammation, leads to altered body composition and diminished function."],"answer":3,"exp":"Malnutrition is defined broadly as a nutrition state (acute, subacute, or chronic) combining varying degrees of over- or under-nutrition, with or without inflammation, that changes body composition and diminishes function. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which strategy is listed to help increase satiety and control hunger, per lecture?","choices":["Eating quickly to finish before hunger signals arrive","Skipping meals entirely to reduce daily total caloric intake","Deliberately avoiding all water intake between scheduled meals","Eating high-fiber, high-volume foods rich in water, and eating slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"High-fiber snacks, high-volume foods rich in water and fiber, drinking water between meals, lean proteins, and eating slowly are all listed strategies to increase satiety. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Why does the lecture identify the first trimester as the most critical period for potential fetal problems?","choices":["It is a finite window during which cells develop into tissues and organs, making the quality of nutrition especially important.","It is when the fetus doubles in length and gains the greatest amount of weight overall.","It is the period when the fetus can still be affected by toxins, though not as easily as in the later trimesters.","It is the period when iron intake specifically takes priority for the developing fetus."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the first trimester as the most critical period for potential problems because it is a finite window of opportunity for cells to develop into tissue/organ, making nutritional quality most important. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Per the lecture, maternal dietary restrictions during pregnancy increase the risk of which outcome?","choices":["Fetal macrosomia risk","Gestational diabetes","Low fetal birth weight","Childhood obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists poor fetal development and low fetal birth weight as risks of maternal dietary restrictions. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"The lecture explains that total body water decreases with aging largely because lean body mass is replaced by what?","choices":["Bone, which holds more fluid","Additional muscle tissue","Connective tissue that stores extra water","Fat, which holds less fluid than lean tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: as lean mass declines it is replaced by fat, which holds less fluid, lowering body water. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is enteral nutrition?","choices":["Any diet consisting only of liquid nutrition supplements taken by mouth rather than by tube.","A structured diet restricted to fewer than 800 kilocalories consumed per day.","Nutrition delivered directly into the bloodstream intravenously.","Nutrition fed into the GI tract using a special tube, bypassing the mouth."],"answer":3,"exp":"Enteral nutrition is nutrition fed into the GI tract via a special tube, bypassing the mouth. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which factor is ranked as the #1 influence on food choice?","choices":["Environmental sustainability","Marketing and advertising","Overall cost and economics","Flavor, texture, and appearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture explicitly ranks flavor, texture, and appearance as the #1 influence on food choice, ahead of the other listed influences. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"Which drug-class pairing is correct, according to the lecture?","choices":["Famotidine (Pepcid) — H2 blocker","Cimetidine (Tagamet) — proton pump inhibitor","Omeprazole (Prilosec) — H2 blocker","Esomeprazole (Nexium) — antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists Famotidine (Pepcid) as an example of an H2 blocker. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which food source provides the long-chain omega-3 forms DHA and EPA directly, rather than requiring the body to convert them?","choices":["Canola oil, a plant-based source","Chia and hemp seeds, both plant-based","Flaxseed and walnuts, both plant-based","Fatty fish such as salmon and mackerel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fatty fish (salmon, tuna, mackerel, lake trout) and fish oil directly supply DHA and EPA, the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids; about 2 servings of fish per week is the recommended intake. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 56 (Omega-3 Fatty Acid Sources)"},{"q":"How does lecture characterize obesity as a medical condition?","choices":["A condition that always resolves completely on its own without any medical intervention.","A condition entirely unrelated to any identifiable or treatable medical process.","A chronic disease requiring sustainable lifestyle changes, not short-term crash diets.","A temporary lifestyle inconvenience carrying no long-term medical significance."],"answer":2,"exp":"Obesity is described as a chronic disease requiring sustainable lifestyle changes — not the short-term, rapid weight loss seen in crash diets, which is the primary reason so many people regain lost weight. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"According to the lecture, the eating pattern shared across the Blue Zones most closely resembles which diet?","choices":["A high-protein carnivore diet","A very-low-carbohydrate ketogenic diet","The Mediterranean diet","The Standard American Diet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Blue Zones follow a Mediterranean-style pattern with the lowest rates of chronic disease. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is the first step in diagnosing a food allergy?","choices":["Skin testing performed first in a clinical allergy specialist's office","Elimination diet, starting with low-allergy foods like rice, vegetables, and fresh meats","Blood test for antibodies against specific food antigens","Food challenge with the suspected allergen reintroduced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture identifies the elimination diet as the first step in food allergy diagnosis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What happens to the small intestine in celiac disease, according to the lecture?","choices":["Villi are flattened, limiting nutrient absorption","Pouches form and protrude through the intestinal wall","The lining erodes, forming ulcers","Veins become swollen and prone to bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states celiac disease flattens villi and limits absorption of nutrients. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does \"chain-breaking\" refer to as a behavioral weight-loss strategy, per lecture?","choices":["A specific type of high-intensity resistance-training exercise routine performed several times weekly.","Separating behaviors that tend to occur together, such as overindulging on chips while watching television.","Deliberately breaking an extended fast after a lengthy period of intentional caloric restriction.","Breaking down one large meal into several smaller courses served throughout the evening."],"answer":1,"exp":"Chain-breaking separates behaviors that tend to occur together, such as overindulging on potato chips while watching television, to disrupt the automatic pairing. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"According to lecture, what degree of lean body mass loss leads to impaired cardiac function, compromised respiratory function, and increased pneumonia risk?","choices":["A loss of 5% lean mass","A loss of 15% lean mass","A loss of 10% lean mass","Loss of 30% lean body mass"],"answer":3,"exp":"A loss of 30% lean body mass leads to impaired cardiac function, compromised respiratory function, increased pneumonia risk, and spontaneous wounds like pressure ulcers. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A food's \"energy density\" is determined by comparing which two properties?","choices":["Its fiber content and its vitamin content.","Its macronutrient ratio and its price.","Its sugar content and its sodium content.","Its kilocalorie content and its weight."],"answer":3,"exp":"Energy density compares a food's calorie (kcal) content with its weight — foods like salsa or vegetable soup are low-energy-density because they pack few calories per gram, while foods like butter are high-energy-density. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 51 (Energy Density)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, a deficiency of which micronutrient increases the risk of osteoporosis and muscle weakness, with recommended intake rising to 800 international units daily at age 70 and older?","choices":["Vitamin D","Vitamin E","Zinc","Iron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: vitamin D deficiency raises osteoporosis and muscle-weakness risk, with 800 international units recommended at age 70 and older. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Per the metric conversions given in lecture, about how many milliliters are in one teaspoon?","choices":["240 mL","5 mL","30 mL","15 mL"],"answer":1,"exp":"One teaspoon is about 5 milliliters, per lecture's metric summary. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 27 (Metric System Summary)"},{"q":"According to the lecture's fish consumption guidelines, what is defined as one serving of fish for a pregnant woman?","choices":["2 ounces","4 ounces","6 ounces","8 ounces"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines a serving of fish as 4 ounces. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which weight-loss medication is described as slowing gastric emptying and enhancing satiety, per lecture?","choices":["Liraglutide (Saxenda®)","Contrave (bupropion/naltrexone)","Phentermine (Adipex-P®)","Orlistat (Xenical®)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Liraglutide (Saxenda®) slows gastric emptying and enhances satiety. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is refeeding syndrome?","choices":["A specific surgical complication of gastric bypass procedures, caused by anastomotic leakage and localized infection at the staple line.","An IgE-mediated hypersensitivity allergic reaction triggered by protein components in standard enteral tube-feeding formula, causing hives and anaphylaxis.","A completely normal, expected, and harmless part of resuming a full oral diet after any illness, surgery, or hospitalization.","A metabolic complication when nutrition support is given to severely malnourished patients, where a shift from catabolism to anabolism drives electrolytes into cells."],"answer":3,"exp":"Refeeding syndrome is a metabolic complication triggered when nutrition support is administered to severely malnourished patients — the shift from catabolism to anabolism (and dextrose to insulin) drives electrolytes into the cells. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which first solid food does the lecture specifically suggest introducing, and in what serving size at first?","choices":["Whole cow's milk, starting with 4 ounces","Honey, starting with 1 tablespoon","Mixed table foods, a full bowl at a time","Iron-fortified rice cereal, starting with a 1-teaspoon serving"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: iron-fortified cereals such as rice cereal are suggested, starting with a 1-teaspoon serving. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 243"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which foods should a patient start with when beginning an elimination diet for suspected food allergy?","choices":["High-protein foods such as red meat, shellfish, and eggs","Low allergy foods such as rice, vegetables, and fresh meats","Dairy-based foods used to establish a symptom baseline","A variety of common allergens to identify triggers quickly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states the elimination diet should 'start out eating low allergy foods (rice, vegetables, and fresh meats).' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Per the lecture's definition, a 36-year-old woman would be classified as experiencing infertility after how many months of unprotected intercourse without conception?","choices":["6 months","12 months","3 months","18 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines infertility as inability to conceive after 6 months for women 35 years or older, and this patient is 36. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What micronutrients matter most for pressure wound healing, per lecture?","choices":["Zinc, vitamin A, and vitamin C","Vitamin B12, folate, and iron","Sodium, potassium, and magnesium","Iron, vitamin D, and calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Zinc, vitamin A, and vitamin C are the micronutrients emphasized for pressure wound healing (with a caution not to over-supplement vitamin A, which can cause toxicity). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which definition best matches the lecture's definition of \"nutrition\"?","choices":["The process of digesting and absorbing macronutrients.","The regulation of body temperature through diet.","The measurement of body fat percentage.","The science that links foods to health."],"answer":3,"exp":"Nutrition is defined in lecture as the science that links foods to health — food provides both energy (calories) and the compounds needed to build and maintain body cells. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 5 (Nutrition)"},{"q":"What neurological complication can result from untreated thiamine deficiency during refeeding?","choices":["Bell's palsy with unilateral facial nerve paralysis","Wernicke's and Korsakoff encephalopathy","Trigeminal neuralgia with episodic facial pain","Guillain-Barré syndrome with ascending muscle paralysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Untreated thiamine deficiency during refeeding can lead to Wernicke's and Korsakoff encephalopathy. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The lecture states roughly what percentage of adults over 65 have chronic conditions such as cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, cancers, and osteoporosis?","choices":["About 50%","Fewer than 10%","About 80% or more","About 20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 80% or more of adults over 65 have such chronic conditions, many of which can be prevented or managed. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"During the adolescent growth spurt, the lecture notes girls and boys gain body tissue differently. Which is correct?","choices":["Girls gain fat and lean tissue, while boys gain mainly lean tissue","Both girls and boys gain mainly fat tissue","Girls gain only lean tissue and boys gain only fat","Neither gains any tissue during the spurt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: girls gain both fat and lean tissue, whereas boys gain mainly lean tissue. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 514"},{"q":"Which warning sign is listed as characteristic of an unreliable (\"fad\") diet, per lecture?","choices":["Encourages gradual weight loss over many months.","Encourages regular physical activity alongside dietary changes.","Recommends a registered dietitian nutritionist for guidance.","Promotes rapid weight loss and uses personal testimonials as evidence."],"answer":3,"exp":"Fad diets typically promote rapid weight loss, limit or restrict food selections, use personal testimonials, claim to be a cure-all for everyone, and recommend expensive supplements. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which body tissue can use only glucose for energy, unlike the brain, which can switch to ketone bodies if necessary?","choices":["Liver","Skeletal muscle","Red blood cells","Adipose tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Red blood cells can only use glucose for energy. The brain prefers glucose but can use ketone bodies (partial fat-breakdown products) when necessary. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 30 (Provide Energy)"},{"q":"Which type of fiber is specifically linked to reducing cholesterol absorption in the gut?","choices":["Functional fiber added to processed foods","Insoluble fiber (e.g., lignin, cellulose)","Soluble/viscous fiber (e.g., pectin, gums)","Resistant starch fiber exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Soluble (viscous) fiber — gum, pectin, carrageenan, mucilage, found in beans, oats, some fruits/vegetables, rice bran, and psyllium — reduces cholesterol absorption and cholesterol synthesis in the liver. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 14 (Fiber: Reducing Cholesterol Absorption); contrast slides 9-10"},{"q":"What waist circumference defines abdominal (android) obesity in men, per lecture?","choices":["Greater than 50 inches","Greater than 40 inches","Greater than 30 inches","Greater than 35 inches"],"answer":1,"exp":"A waist circumference greater than 40 inches defines abdominal (android) obesity in men. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Per the metric conversions in the lecture, about how many grams are in one pound?","choices":["About 1,000 grams","About 100 grams","About 2.2 grams","About 454 grams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: one pound is about 454 grams. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26"},{"q":"The lecture notes soft drinks replacing milk contributes to low intake of which nutrients among teenagers?","choices":["Iron and vitamin B12","Fiber and potassium","Calcium and vitamin D","Sodium and added sugar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: soft drinks replacing milk contribute to low calcium and vitamin D intake in teenagers. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 535"},{"q":"Which of the following is a 2019 AAP recommendation for food allergy prevention through diet, according to the lecture?","choices":["Delaying introduction of allergenic foods beyond 6 months of age","Maternal dietary restriction of allergens during pregnancy","Extended breastfeeding for a specific minimum duration","Early introduction of peanut protein between 4 and 6 months of age"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states there is evidence that early introduction of peanut protein between 4 and 6 months of age reduces the risk for peanut allergies. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"The lecture notes iron-deficiency anemia in teenagers may appear during growth spurts and that which group may need a multivitamin with iron?","choices":["Menstruating teenage girls","All teenage boys","Only infants under 6 months","Teenagers who avoid all dairy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture notes menstruating teenage girls may need a multivitamin with iron. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 544"}]};
